# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# ⚠️⚠️ THIS IS A **RECORD**, NOT AN ENFORCEMENT. NOTHING DEPLOYS THIS FILE.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# ⚠️ WHAT THIS IS. A verbatim copy of the LIVE `public_html/.htaccess` on the production host
# (Hostinger shared, `~/domains/vyrobel.com/public_html/.htaccess`), recorded here on 2026-09-21.
#
# ⚠️⚠️ WHY IT EXISTS. That file is the only thing standing between the public internet and `.env`,
# `artisan` and `storage/` — because the document root IS the application root (see KNOWN-LIMITS L12p).
# Until this copy was made it existed in exactly ONE place, with its only backup
# (`~/htaccess-backups/`) sitting **on the same disk as the file it protects**. ⚠️ That is not a backup,
# it is a second copy of the same risk — the identical mistake this project already made with the git
# bundle before the push. This repository is pushed, so this is the first copy of this file that has
# ever existed OFF the host.
#
# ⚠️⚠️ IT IS NOT LIVE AND EDITING IT CHANGES NOTHING. `public_html` is a real directory the hosting
# panel owns; `ops/deploy.sh` never writes there, and a deploy moves only the `current` symlink. So:
#   · Editing this file does NOT change production.
#   · Deploying does NOT restore production from this file.
#   · Restoring is a MANUAL step: copy everything below the BEGIN marker to
#     `~/domains/vyrobel.com/public_html/.htaccess`, then re-run `ops/exposure-battery.sh`.
# ⚠️ A file in a repository that LOOKS deployed and is not is worse than no file at all, because the
# next person assumes it is live. It is not. This block is why you know that.
#
# ⚠️ TWO LEVELS OF DEFENCE, AND THEY CATCH DIFFERENT THINGS:
#   · `ops/htaccess-drift-check.sh` catches a CHANGE  — the rules stopped being present.
#   · `ops/exposure-battery.sh`     catches an EFFECT — something became reachable.
# ⚠️ A rule can be edited without anything becoming reachable, and something can become reachable
# without this file changing (a panel change, a server that stops honouring AllowOverride). Neither
# check subsumes the other — the same reason the alarm carries two independent detectors.
#
#
# ⚠️⚠️ UPDATED 2026-09-23 (LIVE 01) — THE CANONICAL CONTENT NOW CARRIES THE PRIVATE-PREVIEW LOCK.
# The body below is no longer the 2026-09-21 file: it gained a Basic Auth block with three anchored
# exemptions. ⚠️ THE LOCK IS USELESS WITHOUT `~/domains/vyrobel.com/.htpasswd`, WHICH IS NOT IN THIS
# REPOSITORY AND MUST NOT BE — it is the founder's own password, typed on the server. ⚠️ Restoring this
# file onto a host with no `.htpasswd` makes Apache return **500 on every non-exempt path**, which is a
# louder failure than a leak but is still an outage: create the password file FIRST, then restore.
#
# 🔬 Recorded sha256 of the canonical content below: 55f21c444cb3ff4976a9da4384e76025821c20d777867839454bf05745e154c0
#
# ───────── BEGIN CANONICAL CONTENT — restore from the NEXT line down ─────────
# ── Vyrobel — serve the CURRENT release through ONE pointer ────────────────────
# ⚠️ `public_html` stays a real directory the hosting panel owns. A deploy moves only the
#    `current` symlink below it: ONE atomic rename, with nothing beside it that must also move.
# ⚠️⚠️ FollowSymLinks is REQUIRED — a symlink INSIDE the served tree is not traversed without
#    it, which is why the first attempt 404'd every application route.
Options +FollowSymLinks -Indexes

RewriteEngine On

RewriteCond %{THE_REQUEST} \s/current/public/(\S*)\sHTTP
RewriteRule ^ /%1 [R=301,L]

# ⚠️⚠️ The front controller AND every build asset resolve THROUGH `current`, so a release
#    swap is one operation and assets can never lag a release behind the code.
# ⚠️ The site root goes STRAIGHT to the front controller. Rewriting it to a DIRECTORY lets
#    Laravel's own "redirect trailing slashes" rule fire on /current/public/ and emit a 301 — which a
#    CDN then caches. 🔬 Measured 2026-09-20: that 301 was replayed for ~30 minutes after the origin was
#    correct again. One explicit rule removes the interaction entirely.
RewriteRule ^$ /current/public/index.php [L]

RewriteCond %{REQUEST_URI} !^/current/public
RewriteRule ^(.*)$ /current/public/$1 [L]

<FilesMatch "^\.">
    Require all denied
</FilesMatch>
RedirectMatch 404 ^/(bootstrap|config|database|resources|routes|storage|vendor|ops)(/|$)
RedirectMatch 404 ^/current/(app|bootstrap|config|database|resources|routes|storage|vendor|ops)(/|$)
RedirectMatch 404 ^/current/(composer\.(json|lock)|package\.json|artisan|vite\.config\.js)$
RedirectMatch 404 ^/(composer\.(json|lock)|package\.json|artisan|vite\.config\.js)$

# ── ⚠️⚠️ THE PRIVATE-PREVIEW LOCK — added 2026-09-23 (LIVE 01) ──────────────────────────────────────
# ⚠️⚠️ THIS REPLACES MAINTENANCE MODE AS THE WAY THE SITE STAYS PRIVATE, AND THE REASON IS THE QUEUE.
#    🔬 `queue:work` PAUSES while the app is down (measured DEPLOY 06, re-measured REPAIR 01: a heartbeat
#    sat 55 hours and drained within 60 s of an unpark). Under maintenance every booking would queue a job
#    that never runs, so nothing anyone tested would behave like production.
#    ⚠️ THE GOAL IS A LOCK ON THE DOOR, NOT A CLOSED SIGN.
#
# ⚠️ WHY BASIC AUTH AND NOT AN IP ALLOWLIST: a CDN terminates the connection (`server: hcdn`, measured
#    DEPLOY 09), so Apache may see the EDGE's address and not the visitor's — and whether this host
#    restores the real client IP could not be measured without serving a probe file on a live public path.
#    ⚠️ A lock whose correctness rests on an unmeasured variable is the wrong lock. Basic Auth also
#    survives his network changing, which an IP rule does not.
#
# ⚠️⚠️ NO PASSWORD IS IN THIS FILE AND NONE EVER WAS. `.htpasswd` is written by the founder, on the
#    server, with a password no session has chosen, generated, logged or transmitted.
# ⚠️ AuthUserFile SITS OUTSIDE THE DOCUMENT ROOT ON PURPOSE — `public_html` IS the application root here
#    (KNOWN-LIMITS L12p), so a password file inside it would be one `.htaccess` mistake from being served.
#
# ── THE EXEMPTIONS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ⚠️⚠️ SetEnvIf RUNS AT HEADER-PARSE, BEFORE mod_rewrite — so `Request_URI` is the path the CALLER asked
#    for, not the rewritten `/current/public/...`. ⚠️ BUT an internal redirect re-runs authorization on
#    the NEW uri and renames every env var with a `REDIRECT_` prefix, so BOTH spellings are matched and
#    BOTH names are accepted below. 🔬 Proven from outside, not reasoned about: see LIVE 01's measurements.
#
# ⚠️⚠️ EVERY PATTERN IS ANCHORED (`$`). A bare `^/webhooks/` prefix would exempt `/webhooks/../anything`,
#    and ON A MONEY PATH AN OVER-PERMISSIVE CHECK *IS* THE BUG. These five are the whole exempt list, and
#    each one is a route that `route:list` really serves — measured, not assumed.
#    ⚠️ Request_URI excludes the query string, so `?cb=…` cache-busting cannot widen these.
#
#   · POST /webhooks/stripe · POST /webhooks/stripe/connect
#       ⚠️⚠️ A MACHINE CANNOT TYPE A PASSWORD. And because Stripe is wired but not yet calling, a blocked
#       webhook would be SILENT FOR WEEKS and would surface as a reconciliation mystery — the quietest
#       possible failure, which is exactly why it is exempted before anybody needs it.
#   · POST /webhooks/channex/{channel} — same reason; the channel manager is a machine too.
#   · GET /up · GET /up/liveness
#       ⚠️ The external dead-man's-switch polls these. Locking them makes the alarm fail for the wrong
#       reason, which is worse than no alarm because it teaches people to ignore it.
#   · ⚠️ NO POSTMARK INBOUND OR BOUNCE ROUTE EXISTS — 🔬 measured against `route:list`, not assumed.
#       Nothing to exempt today. If one is ever added it belongs on this list in the same change.
#
# ⚠️ STATIC ASSETS ARE DELIBERATELY *INSIDE* THE LOCK. One origin means the browser replays credentials
#    for CSS/JS automatically, so the app renders normally; the cost is that the CDN caches nothing while
#    the preview is private. That is the right trade for a site with one intended visitor.
SetEnvIf Request_URI "^(/current/public)?/webhooks/stripe(/connect)?/?$" VY_PUBLIC=1
SetEnvIf Request_URI "^(/current/public)?/webhooks/channex/[A-Za-z0-9._-]+/?$" VY_PUBLIC=1
SetEnvIf Request_URI "^(/current/public)?/up(/liveness)?/?$" VY_PUBLIC=1

AuthType Basic
AuthName "Vyrobel - private preview"
AuthUserFile /home/u545270279/domains/vyrobel.com/.htpasswd
<RequireAny>
    Require env VY_PUBLIC
    Require env REDIRECT_VY_PUBLIC
    Require valid-user
</RequireAny>
